</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Tab. <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 3.2.4 Comparisons among Different Retrieval Query Construction Methods. ‣ 3.2 Evaluation Results 17 footnote 17 17 footnote 17 The results for CCEval are reported in Appendix . ‣ 3 Experiment ‣ CodeRAG: Finding Relevant and Necessary Knowledge for Retrieval-Augmented Repository-Level Code Completion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we compare the retrieval query construction method used in <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">CodeRAG</span> and other retrieval query construction methods.
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CodeRAG}_{\text{jacc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>CodeRAG</mtext><mtext>jacc</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CodeRAG}_{\text{jacc}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents constructing the retrieval query by selecting the chunk <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that is most similar to the target block (Alg. <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 2.2 Retrieval Query Construction via Log Probability Guided Probing ‣ 2 Our Method CodeRAG ‣ CodeRAG: Finding Relevant and Necessary Knowledge for Retrieval-Augmented Repository-Level Code Completion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) w.r.t. Jaccard similarity when using sparse retrieval.
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CodeRAG}_{\text{last\_k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>CodeRAG</mtext><mtext>last_k</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CodeRAG}_{\text{last\_k}}</annotation></semantics></math> means constructing the retrieval query with the last <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> lines when using sparse retrieval.
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CodeRAG}_{\text{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>CodeRAG</mtext><mtext>s</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CodeRAG}_{\text{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates using our retrieval query construction method in sparse retrieval.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From the results in Tab. <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 3.2.4 Comparisons among Different Retrieval Query Construction Methods. ‣ 3.2 Evaluation Results 17 footnote 17 17 footnote 17 The results for CCEval are reported in Appendix . ‣ 3 Experiment ‣ CodeRAG: Finding Relevant and Necessary Knowledge for Retrieval-Augmented Repository-Level Code Completion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we can observe that our retrieval query construction method significantly outperforms other simple methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.5 </span>Computational Cost</h4>

<figure id="S3.T6" class="ltx_table">
